🛡️ Safeguarding GCP Infrastructure with Terraform
This documentation outlines the standards for managing Google Cloud Service Account credentials securely using .env, variables.tf, and provider.tf.

1. Objective: The "Least Privilege" Principle

The primary objective for GCP safeguarding is to protect the Service Account Key.

Key Protection: Service Account keys are powerful JSON files; if leaked, they provide full access to your project.

Path Referencing: We store the path to the key in an environment variable, not the key's content in the code.

Identity Isolation: Ensures the "Editor" or "Owner" permissions are only active during the Terraform execution.

2. Why We Use These Files

📂 .env (The Path Controller)

In GCP, we often point to a local file path.
The .env file exports this path so Terraform knows where to find the "passport" (JSON key) for Google Cloud.

📂 variables.tf (The Blueprint)

This file defines the project-specific variables such as project_id and region. 
It ensures your code isn't hardcoded to one specific GCP project.

📂 provider.tf (The Bridge)

This file connects to the Google API. 
It uses the variables to define where to build resources and how to authenticate.

3. Implementation & Code Syntax

📂 File 1: .env

Note: Ensure your Service Account JSON file is also added to .gitignore.

# .env - DO NOT COMMIT TO GIT

export TF_VAR_project_id="your-gcp-project-id"

export TF_VAR_region="us-central1"

export TF_VAR_gcp_auth_file="/path/to/your/service-account-key.json"

# Best Practice: Also set the standard Google Variable

export GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS="/path/to/your/service-account-key.json"

📂 File 2: variables.tf

variable "project_id" {
  description = "The ID of the GCP Project"
  type        = string
}

variable "region" {
  description = "The default region for resources"
  type        = string
  default     = "us-central1"
}

variable "gcp_auth_file" {
  description = "Path to the Service Account JSON key"
  type        = string
}

📂 File 3: provider.tf
provider "google" {
  project     = var.project_id
  region      = var.region
  credentials = file(var.gcp_auth_file)
}

4. How to Connect the Files (The Workflow)

Load Environment:

source .env

Plan:

terraform plan

⚠️ Critical Security Step

Your .gitignore must be strict to prevent JSON key leakage:


# Ignore the environment file
.env

# Ignore all Google JSON keys
*.json

# Ignore Terraform internal files
.terraform/
*.tfstate


Seucrity Question:

Q) If the .json key path is already hidden inside the .gitignore-ed .env file, why must we also explicitly ignore *.json files?

A) While it may seem redundant, ignoring both is a "Defense in Depth" strategy. Here is the technical breakdown of why this is a best practice:

1. Separating the "Map" from the "Treasure"

The .env file is just a "Map": It contains the text-based path (the address) where your key is stored on your hard drive.

The .json file is the "Treasure": This is the actual physical file containing your private RSA keys.

The Risk: If you only ignore the .env file, Git is still free to track any other file in your folder. 
If you accidentally move your gcpKey.json into your project directory to make it "easier to find," Git will see it as a new untracked file and include it in your next git add . command.

2. Preventing Human Error (The "Accidental Add")Developers often use the command git add . to stage all changes. 
Without *.json in your .gitignore, a service account key sitting in your root folder will be staged and committed immediately. 
Once a key is pushed to a remote repository, it is considered compromised—even if you delete it in a later commit, it remains in the Git History.

3. Protection Against Local ScriptsSome automated tools or scripts might generate temporary JSON outputs or logs containing sensitive data. A wildcard ignore like *.json acts as a safety net that catches these files before they ever leave your machine.

